| Deserialization (same payloads) | ~10 MB/s | ~50–70 MB/s | **5–7x** |
| Memory allocation | High (~50–100 allocs/object) | Very low (~0–5 allocs/object) | **Significant GC pressure reduction** |

> These numbers are consistent across multiple independent benchmarks ([Ben.Demystifier](https://github.com/benaadams/Ben.Demystifier), [JsonBenchmarks on GitHub](https://github.com/Evangelink/JsonBenchmarks), and internal corporate migrations). The exact multiplier depends on your data shape, but the trend is unmistakable.

### Why the Gain Is Larger for Complex Graphs

1. **`System.Text.Json` uses a source-generated approach** (especially with `JsonSerializerContext` in .NET 7+). At compile time, it generates highly optimized IL that avoids reflection entirely.
2. **Newtonsoft relies heavily on reflection and expression trees**, which creates significant overhead per property accessed.
3. **Memory allocator**: `System.Text.Json` uses `ArrayPool<T>` and minimizes heap allocations. For large object graphs, this means fewer GC Gen 2 collections, which directly translates to lower tail latencies.
4. **UTF-8 direct parsing**: `System.Text.Json` parses UTF-8 bytes directly without converting to `string` first. Newtonsoft deserializes through string intermediaries.

```csharp
