## Caveats & Gotchas You Must Consider

### 1. Feature Parity Gaps

| Feature | Newtonsoft | System.Text.Json (.NET 7) |
|---|---|---|
| Custom converters | ✅ Rich ecosystem | ✅ Supported, but less intuitive |
| Polymorphic serialization (`TypeNameHandling`) | ✅ Built-in | ⚠️ Requires custom converter or `[JsonDerivedType]` (.NET 7+) |
| Ignoring null values | ✅ `[DefaultValueHandling]` | ✅ `DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull` |
| Case-insensitive property matching | ✅ Configurable | ✅ `PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase` + ignore case option |
| Reference loops / `$id`/$ref | ✅ Native support | ❌ No native circular reference handling until .NET 7 (`ReferenceHandler.Preserve`) |
| Date/time formats | ✅ Flexible (`IsoDateTimeConverter`, etc.) | ⚠️ Limited flexibility; may need custom converters |
| Dynamic/ExpandoObject | ✅ Works well | ❌ Not supported natively |
| Comments in JSON | ✅ Can preserve/ignore | ⚠️ Must explicitly enable `AllowTrailingCommas = true` |

### 2. Migration Pain Points

```csharp
// Newtonsoft behavior that breaks silently in System.Text.Json:

// 1. Case-insensitive deserialization by default? NOT in STJ.
//    Newtonsoft: { "UserName": "bob" } → UserName = "bob"
//    STJ: Requires exact match unless you configure PropertyNameCaseInsensitive

// 2. Null handling differences
//    Newtonsoft: Ignores missing properties gracefully
//    STJ: May throw or set defaults differently depending on configuration

// 3. Circular references
//    Newtonsoft: Handles them with PreserveReferencesHandling
//    STJ (.NET 7): Supports via ReferenceHandler.Preserve, but output format differs ($id/$ref structure)

// 4. Custom type resolution for polymorphism
//    Newtonsoft: TypeNameHandling.All works everywhere
//    STJ: Requires [JsonDerivedType] attributes or custom converters — 
//          must annotate ALL derived types at compile time
