| Operation | Newtonsoft.Json (.NET Framework 4.8) | System.Text.Json (.NET 7, default) | System.Text.Json (.NET 7, source-gen) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Serialize (ms/1k objects) | ~145 ms | ~62 ms | ~38 ms |
| Deserialize (ms/1k objects) | ~178 ms | ~75 ms | ~48 ms |
| Relative speedup (vs Newton) | 1.0x | ~2.3x serialize, ~2.4x deserialize | ~3.8x serialize, ~3.7x deserialize |
| Gen 2 GC pressure (MB) | ~8.5 MB | ~4.2 MB | ~2.8 MB |

> **Key observation**: The absolute time savings grow with object size and request volume. In an API handling 10,000 requests/sec, this translates to **hundreds of milliseconds saved per second in total CPU time**, which matters for cloud cost and latency percentiles.

### Scenario 3: Edge Cases Where Newtonsoft Still Leads

| Case | Newtonsoft Advantage | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Polymorphic serialization without explicit converters | Slightly simpler setup | `TypeNameHandling` works out-of-the-box; STJ requires custom converters or source-gen limitations |
| Handling malformed/invalid JSON gracefully | More forgiving | STJ throws on many edge cases (trailing commas, comments) unless explicitly configured |
| Custom contract resolvers | More flexible | Newtonsoft's `IContractResolver` model is richer for dynamic schema changes |
| Very small payloads (< 1KB) | Negligible difference | Overhead dominates; difference is < 1ms per operation |

---

## Source Generation: The Game-Changer

This is where .NET 7 truly pulls ahead. With source generators:

```csharp
[JsonSerializable(typeof(Order))]
[JsonSerializable(typeof(User))]
public partial class MyContext : JsonSerializerContext { }

// Usage
var json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(order, MyContext.Default.Order);
var order = JsonSerializer.Deserialize(json, MyContext.Default.Order);
```

**Benefits:**
- Zero reflection at runtime
- Up to **3–5x faster** than non-source-gen STJ
- Compile-time validation of serialization compatibility
- Works seamlessly with complex graphs *if* all types are declared upfront

**Limitations:**
- Requires knowing all serialized types at compile time
- Doesn't work well with open generics or fully dynamic schemas
- Adds build-time complexity

---

## Migration Considerations Beyond Performance

### Breaking Behavioral Differences

| Area | Newtonsoft | System.Text.Json |
|---|---|---|
| Date format | ISO 8601 with milliseconds by default | RFC 3339 (similar but subtle differences in round-tripping) |
| Null handling | Ignores nulls if configured | Includes nulls by default; configurable via `DefaultIgnoreCondition` |
| Case sensitivity | Configurable case-insensitive matching | Case-sensitive by default |
| Property naming | Flexible camelCase/PascalCase configs | Similar, but defaults differ |
| Reference loops | Handles with `PreserveReferencesHandling` | Requires `ReferenceHandler.Preserve` (limited support in source-gen) |
| Comments in JSON | Supported | Not supported by default (must enable `JsonCommentHandling.Skip`) |

### Common Migration Pain Points

1. **Custom JsonConverter implementations** must be rewritten. The converter API is fundamentally different.
2. **Conditional property serialization** (`[JsonProperty(NullValueHandling = NullValueHandling.Ignore)]`) maps to `[JsonIgnore(Condition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull)]`.
3. **Polymorphic deserialization** requires writing custom `JsonConverterFactory` or using `JsonDerivedType` attributes (new in .NET 7).
4. **Dynamic/object types** lose performance benefits; STJ falls back to reflection-heavy paths for `object` and `Dictionary<string, object>` unless carefully structured.
5. **Third-party libraries** depending on Newtonsoft may need adapter layers.

