}
```

| Approach | Newtonsoft.Json | System.Text.Json (.NET 7) | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| With `[JsonConverter(typeof(StringEnumConverter))]` + manual discriminators | Baseline | **Comparable or slightly slower** initially | STJ needs proper configuration |
| With `[JsonDerivedType]` attributes | N/A (Newtonsoft uses converters) | **~2–3x faster** than Newtonsoft equivalent | Requires .NET 7+ attribute support |
| With source generators | N/A | **~5–8x faster** than Newtonsoft | Best case, but requires build-time setup |

> ⚠️ **This is where migrations trip up.** If you relied heavily on Newtonsoft's `TypeNameHandling.All` or custom `IContractResolver` logic, STJ doesn't have a direct 1:1 replacement out of the box. You'll need to invest time in configuring `JsonSerializerOptions` properly. Once configured correctly, however, STJ pulls ahead.

---

## Where System.Text.Json Falls Short (and Newtonsoft Still Wins)

### 1. **Feature Gaps That Require Workarounds**

| Feature | Newtonsoft.Json | System.Text.Json (.NET 7) | Effort to Migrate |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ignore null values by default | ✅ `NullValueHandling.Ignore` | ✅ `DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull` | Low |
| Custom date formats globally | ✅ `DateFormatString` | ❌ Per-property or converter needed | Medium |
| `$ref` / circular reference handling | ✅ Built-in | ❌ No native support; requires external library | High |
| JSON Patch (`AddProperty`, etc.) | ✅ Full `JObject` manipulation API | ❌ Limited; no mutable document model | High if you mutate documents |
| Dynamic object binding | ✅ `JObject` as dynamic dictionary | ❌ `JsonDocument` is read-only; `JsonElement` navigation is cumbersome | Medium-High |
| Case-insensitive property matching | ✅ Default behavior | ❌ Opt-in via `PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true` | Low |
| Handling invalid JSON gracefully | ✅ Lenient by default | ❌ Strict by default; throws on minor issues | Medium |

### 2. **The "Mutable Document" Problem**

If your legacy code does things like:

```csharp
// Newtonsoft pattern — very common in legacy apps
var token = JToken.Parse(json);
token["Orders"][0]["Status"] = "SHIPPED";
token.Add("ProcessedAt", DateTime.UtcNow);
return token.ToString();
```

There is **no direct equivalent** in `System.Text.Json`. You'd need to:
- Deserialize into POCOs, modify, and re-serialize (adds round-trip cost), OR
- Use `System.Text.Json.Nodes.JsonNode` (available since .NET 6), which provides a mutable document model but is **significantly slower** than the serializer/deserializer path

```csharp
// .NET 7 alternative — works but slower than pure serialize/deserialize
var node = JsonNode.Parse(json);
node["Orders"][0]["Status"] = "SHIPPED";
node["ProcessedAt"] = DateTime.UtcNow;
return node.ToJsonString();
```

In benchmarks, `JsonNode` operations are roughly **5–10x slower** than `JToken` equivalents because they were not designed for high-performance mutation.

